.',explain_glycemia:'Blood sugar level. Fasting, a normal level is between 0.7 and 1.1 g/L.',explain_bmi:'Weight/height ratio. Normal between 18.5 and 25. Above 25: overweight.',explain_visceral_fat:'Fat around internal organs. An index below 10 is healthy.',explain_basal_metabolism:'Energy spent at rest to maintain vital functions.',explain_recommended_calories:'Calories to consume daily to maintain your current weight.',explain_waist_hip:'Fat distribution indicator. Below 0.90 (men) or 0.85 (women) is ideal.',explain_body_age:'Estimated biological age based on your body composition.',explain_ideal_weight:'Optimal weight calculated from your height and build.',explain_steps:'Recommended goal: 6,000 to 10,000 steps per day for good health.',explain_calories:'Calories burned through physical activity today.',explain_distance:'Total distance estimated from step count.',explain_vo2:'Maximum aerobic capacity. Higher means better fitness.',explain_stress:'Stress index measured by heart rate variability. Lower is better.',explain_recovery:'Your body\\'s ability to recover. A high score indicates good recovery.',explain_weight:'Your total weight. Interpret with body composition.',explain_body_fat:'Body fat proportion. Normal: 15-25% men, 20-30% women.',explain_muscle:'Muscle proportion. Higher means better metabolism.',explain_water:'Water percentage in the body. Normal between 50% and 65%.',explain_bone_mass:'Bone mineral weight. Important for preventing osteoporosis.',explain_protein:'Protein percentage. Important for muscle repair.',explain_skeletal_muscle:'Muscles attached to bones, responsible for movement.',explain_subcutaneous_fat:'Fat located just under the skin.',explain_trunk_fat:'Fat accumulated in the abdominal region.',rich_hr_desc:\"Resting heart rate measures the number of times your heart beats per minute when calm. It is a fundamental indicator of cardiovascular health.\",rich_hr_why:\"A healthy heart beats less often because it pumps more blood per beat. Athletes often have lower resting HR.\",rich_hr_tip:\"Always measure at rest, in the morning upon waking. Avoid caffeine beforehand. A consistently low HR is a sign of good fitness.\",rich_hr_src:\"WHO, American Heart Association\",rich_hr_r1:'Bradycardia',rich_hr_r2:'Normal',rich_hr_r3:'Elevated',rich_hr_r4:'Tachycardia',rich_spo2_desc:\"Oxygen saturation (SpO2) indicates the percentage of hemoglobin carrying oxygen in your blood. It reflects lung efficiency.\",rich_spo2_why:\"Your organs need a constant supply of oxygen. Low SpO2 may indicate respiratory or cardiac problems.\",rich_spo2_tip:\"At altitude, SpO2 drops naturally. If it falls below 92% at rest at low altitude, consult quickly.\",rich_spo2_src:\"Pulse Oximetry Guidelines, WHO\",rich_spo2_r1:'Critical',rich_spo2_r2:'Low',rich_spo2_r3:'Normal',rich_bp_desc:\"Blood pressure measures the force of blood on artery walls. Systolic (high) corresponds to heart contraction, diastolic (low) to rest.\",rich_bp_why:\"Untreated hypertension silently damages arteries, heart, kidneys and brain over years.\",rich_bp_tip:\"Measure calmly, seated for 5 min. Pressure varies during the day \\u2014 higher in the morning. Reduce salt and stress.\",rich_bp_src:\"ESC/ESH Guidelines 2023\",rich_bp_r1:'Low',rich_bp_r2:'Optimal',rich_bp_r3:'Elevated',rich_bp_r4:'Hypertension',rich_temp_desc:\"Body temperature naturally fluctuates throughout the day. Lower in the morning (~36.2) and peaks in the afternoon (~37.0).\",rich_temp_why:\"Fever is an immune response. Tracking temperature helps detect infections and inflammations early.\",rich_temp_tip:\"After exercise, temperature rises naturally. Wait 30 min before measuring.\",rich_temp_src:\"Internal Medicine, Harrison's\",rich_temp_r1:'Hypothermia',rich_temp_r2:'Normal',rich_temp_r3:'Low-grade fever',rich_temp_r4:'Fever',rich_steps_desc:\"Daily step count is the best simple indicator of physical activity level. WHO recommends 150 min of moderate activity per week, about 7,000-8,000 steps/day.\",rich_steps_why:\"Walking reduces cardiovascular risk by 31%, diabetes by 33%, and improves mood via endorphin release.\",rich_steps_tip:\"For seniors (65+), 6,000 steps/day is enough to reduce mortality by 50%. Increase by 500 steps per week.\",rich_steps_src:\"JAMA Study 2022, Lee et al.\",rich_steps_r1:'Sedentary',rich_steps_r2:'Somewhat active',rich_steps_r3:'Active',rich_steps_r4:'Very active',rich_cal_desc:\"Calories burned through physical activity (excluding basal metabolism). Varies by intensity, duration, and body weight.\",rich_cal_why:\"Burning calories through activity helps maintain a healthy weight, strengthens the immune system and improves sleep.\",rich_cal_tip:\"30 min of brisk walking = ~150 kcal. Consistency is more important than intensity for long-term health.\",rich_cal_src:\"ACSM Guidelines\",rich_cal_r1:'Low',rich_cal_r2:'Moderate',rich_cal_r3:'Active',rich_dist_desc:\"Distance covered during the day reflects your overall mobility. It is an indicator of your autonomy and functional capacity.\",rich_dist_why:\"Maintaining mobility is essential to prevent falls, social isolation, and physical deconditioning.\",rich_dist_tip:\"Vary your routes to stimulate balance and coordination. Walking in nature reduces cortisol by 15%.\",rich_dist_src:\"Journal of Aging & Physical Activity\",rich_dist_r1:'Low',rich_dist_r2:'Moderate',rich_dist_r3:'Good',rich_dist_r4:'Excellent',rich_hrv_desc:\"Heart Rate Variability (HRV) measures beat-to-beat variation. High HRV indicates your nervous system adapts well to stress.\",rich_hrv_why:\"HRV reflects the balance between sympathetic (stress) and parasympathetic (rest) systems. It is a recovery marker.\",rich_hrv_tip:\"HRV is best measured in the morning upon waking. Meditation and deep breathing improve it.\",rich_hrv_src:\"European Society of Cardiology\",rich_hrv_r1:'Low',rich_hrv_r2:'Average',rich_hrv_r3:'Good',rich_hrv_r4:'Excellent',rich_stress_desc:\"Stress level is estimated from HRV, heart rate and skin conductance. It reflects sympathetic nervous system activation.\",rich_stress_why:\"Chronic high stress increases inflammation, blood pressure and risk of depression.\",rich_stress_tip:\"Practice cardiac coherence (5 min, 6 breaths/min) to reduce stress in 3 minutes.\",rich_stress_src:\"HeartMath Institute\",rich_stress_r1:'Relaxed',rich_stress_r2:'Moderate',rich_stress_r3:'Elevated',rich_stress_r4:'Very elevated',rich_recovery_desc:\"Recovery score combines sleep quality, HRV, resting heart rate and stress to assess your exercise capacity.\",rich_recovery_why:\"Training when recovery is low increases injury risk and slows progress.\",rich_recovery_tip:\"If < 50%, favor gentle walking or yoga. Above 80%, you can do intense exercise.\",rich_recovery_src:\"Sports Science, WHOOP Research\",rich_recovery_r1:'Low',rich_recovery_r2:'Moderate',rich_recovery_r3:'Good',rich_recovery_r4:'Optimal',rich_sleep_desc:\"Sleep quality evaluates duration, depth (deep + REM sleep) and continuity of your nights.\",rich_sleep_why:\"Deep sleep repairs tissues and consolidates memory. REM sleep manages emotions and creativity.\",rich_sleep_tip:\"Go to bed and wake up at the same time. Avoid screens 1h before. The room should be cool (18-19 C).\",rich_sleep_src:\"National Sleep Foundation\",rich_sleep_r1:'Poor',rich_sleep_r2:'Average',rich_sleep_r3:'Good',rich_sleep_r4:'Excellent',rich_weight_desc:\"Body weight alone is not enough to assess health. It must be cross-referenced with body composition (fat, muscle, water).\",rich_weight_why:\"Daily variations (0.5-1.5 kg) are normal and related to hydration, digestion and salt.\",rich_weight_tip:\"Always weigh yourself in the morning, fasting, under the same conditions. Track the trend over 2 weeks, not daily variations.\",rich_weight_src:\"Endocrine Society\",rich_bmi_desc:\"Body Mass Index (BMI) relates your weight to your height. It assesses corpulence and risks associated with overweight or underweight.\",rich_bmi_why:\"A high BMI increases cardiovascular, diabetes and osteoarthritis risks. A low BMI weakens bones and immune system.\",rich_bmi_tip:\"BMI does not distinguish fat from muscle mass. Supplement with body composition measurement.\",rich_bmi_src:\"WHO, HAS\",rich_bmi_r1:'Underweight',rich_bmi_r2:'Normal',rich_bmi_r3:'Overweight',rich_bmi_r4:'Obesity',glyc_zone_normal:'Normal',glyc_zone_normal_high:'High normal',glyc_zone_vigilance:'Watch',glyc_zone_prealert:'Pre-alert',glyc_zone_alert:'Alert',glyc_desc_normal:'Healthy glucose metabolism.',glyc_desc_normal_high:'Upper range of normal. Monitoring recommended.',glyc_desc_vigilance:'Potential pre-diabetes. Medical check advised.',glyc_desc_prealert:'High risk. Blood test recommended promptly.',glyc_desc_alert:'Significant risk. Urgent blood test.',glyc_estimation_title:'Estimated Blood Sugar',glyc_estimation_desc:\"Your blood sugar is estimated by combining bracelet data (heart rate, variability, temperature, activity) with an AI model trained on thousands of metabolic profiles. The result is an indicative approximation, not a medical diagnosis.\",glyc_estimation_tip:\"For an exact value, get a fasting blood sugar test prescribed by your doctor. Our estimate improves with each capillary calibration.\",glyc_reliability_title:'Estimation Reliability',glyc_reliability_desc:\"The reliability score reflects the precision of the glycemic estimation. It increases with: the amount of bracelet data, the number of capillary calibrations, and regular bracelet wear. Without calibration, the estimate remains very approximate.\",glyc_reliability_tip:\"Wear your bracelet continuously and perform a capillary calibration (finger prick) once a month. Each calibration significantly improves model accuracy.\",glyc_rel_r1:'Insufficient',glyc_rel_r2:'Initial',glyc_rel_r3:'Improving',glyc_rel_r4:'Reliable',glyc_calibration_title:'Capillary Calibration',glyc_calibration_desc:\"Calibration involves measuring your actual blood sugar via a finger prick (glucose meter). By comparing this real value to bracelet estimates, our algorithm adjusts and refines predictions for your unique profile.\",glyc_calibration_tip:\"Prefer fasting measurements in the morning for reference values. Always indicate context (fasting, after meal) for better calibration. Clean your finger before pricking.\",glyc_cal_r1:'Ideally fasting',glyc_cal_r1v:'Morning',glyc_cal_r2:'After meal',glyc_cal_r2v:'2h after',glyc_cal_r3:'Frequency',glyc_cal_r3v:'1x / month',alert_state_idle:'Waiting',alert_state_calling_patient:'Calling patient',alert_state_calling_guardians:'Calling guardians',alert_state_care_dispatched:'Responder dispatched',alert_state_resolved:'Resolved',alert_type_fall:'Fall detected (vest)',alert_type_manual_app:'SOS Button (app)',alert_type_manual_bracelet:'Manual press (bracelet)',alert_type_sos:'SOS Button',alert_type_health_anomaly:'Health anomaly detected',alert_type_threshold:'Threshold exceeded',alert_type_inactivity:'Inactivity detected',alert_type_geofence:'Safe zone exit',alert_type_default:'Alert',alert_saad_manage:'SAAD Alert Management',alert_understand:'Understanding Alerts',alert_saad_header:'Manage your alerts',alert_saad_intro:\"As a SAAD, you oversee the entire alert process for your beneficiaries, from detection to closure.\",alert_process_header:'The Chutex Care Process',alert_process_intro:\"From alert to resolution, every step is designed to ensure the safety of your loved ones.\",alert_saad_steps_label:'The process from your organization\\'s view',alert_steps_label:'The 5 steps',alert_roles_permissions:'Roles and permissions',alert_saad_actors:'Process stakeholders',alert_faq_title:'Frequently asked questions',alert_role_operator:'Platform Operator',alert_role_intervenant:'Care Responder',alert_role_teleassist:'Remote Assistance',alert_role_operator_a1:'Receive and qualify alerts',alert_role_operator_a2:'Contact the beneficiary',alert_role_operator_a3:'Dispatch a responder',alert_role_intervenant_a1:'Accept an interve